### What is the Strategy pattern?

Strategy replaces a long if-else chain with a set of small classes that all implement one interface. In a banking service, fee calculation differs by customer tier.

```java
package com.bank.strategy;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface FeeStrategy {
    BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal amount);
}

public class RegularFeeStrategy implements FeeStrategy {
    public BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal amount) {
        return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.005")); // 0.5%
    }
}

public class PremiumFeeStrategy implements FeeStrategy {
    public BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal amount) {
        return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.002")); // 0.2%
    }
}

public class ZeroFeeStrategy implements FeeStrategy {
    public BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal amount) {
        return BigDecimal.ZERO; // festival promotion
    }
}
```

Strategies are stored in a Map and selected at runtime:

```java
Map<String, FeeStrategy> strategies = Map.of(
        "regular", new RegularFeeStrategy(),
        "premium", new PremiumFeeStrategy(),
        "zero",    new ZeroFeeStrategy());

FeeStrategy strategy = strategies.get(customerTier);
BigDecimal fee = strategy.calculateFee(new BigDecimal("50000"));
```

Adding a new tier means writing one class and one Map entry, with zero changes to existing code.

### What is the Builder pattern?

Builder constructs an object with many fields step by step, avoiding a constructor with positional arguments where from and to accounts can be swapped.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferResult {
    private final String transferId;
    private final String fromAccount;
    private final String toAccount;
    private final BigDecimal amount;
    private final BigDecimal fee;
    private final String status;

    private TransferResult(Builder b) {
        this.transferId = b.transferId;
        this.fromAccount = b.fromAccount;
        this.toAccount = b.toAccount;
        this.amount = b.amount;
        this.fee = b.fee;
        this.status = b.status;
    }

    public static Builder builder() { return new Builder(); }

    public static class Builder {
        private String transferId, fromAccount, toAccount, status;
        private BigDecimal amount, fee;
        public Builder transferId(String v) { transferId = v; return this; }
        public Builder fromAccount(String v) { fromAccount = v; return this; }
        public Builder toAccount(String v) { toAccount = v; return this; }
        public Builder amount(BigDecimal v) { amount = v; return this; }
        public Builder fee(BigDecimal v) { fee = v; return this; }
        public Builder status(String v) { status = v; return this; }
        public TransferResult build() { return new TransferResult(this); }
    }
}
```

```java
TransferResult result = TransferResult.builder()
        .transferId("TXF-00001")
        .fromAccount("SAV2001")
        .toAccount("CUR3001")
        .amount(new BigDecimal("50000"))
        .fee(new BigDecimal("250"))
        .status("COMPLETED")
        .build();
```

### What is the Observer pattern?

Observer decouples an event from the things that react to it. When a transfer completes, several actions must happen: audit, SMS, dashboard update. The transfer code publishes one event and listeners react independently.

```java
package com.bank.event;

public record TransferCompletedEvent(String transferId, String fromAccount, String toAccount,
                                      java.math.BigDecimal amount) {}
```

```java
// Publisher:
applicationEventPublisher.publishEvent(
        new TransferCompletedEvent("TXF-00001", "SAV2001", "CUR3001", amount));

// Listeners – each independent, never touching the transfer code:
@EventListener
public void onTransfer(TransferCompletedEvent e) {
    auditService.record(e.transferId(), "TRANSFER", "SUCCESS");
}

@EventListener
public void onTransfer(TransferCompletedEvent e) {
    smsService.send(e.fromAccount(), "Transfer " + e.transferId() + " completed.");
}
```

Adding a new reaction is one listener class and never destabilizes the transfer code.

### What is the Circuit Breaker pattern?

Circuit Breaker protects your service from a dependency that has become slow or unavailable. After a threshold of failures it "opens" and returns a fallback immediately.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.annotation.CircuitBreaker;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class FxRateService {

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "fxRate", fallbackMethod = "cachedRate")
    public BigDecimal liveRate(String pair) {
        return externalFxApi.getRate(pair); // may timeout
    }

    private BigDecimal cachedRate(String pair, Throwable t) {
        return lastKnownRate.get(pair); // fallback to cached value
    }
}
```

```text
Closed → calls go through normally
Open   → calls skip the dependency, return fallback immediately
Half-Open → one trial call to test if the dependency recovered
```

### What is the DTO pattern?

DTO separates the data exposed at the API boundary from the entity stored in the database. The entity has internal columns that customers must never see.

```java
// Entity – internal:
@Entity
public class Account {
    @Id private String id;
    private String holderName;
    private String passwordHash;       // never expose
    private int internalRiskScore;     // never expose
    private BigDecimal balance;
}

// DTO – public contract:
public record AccountResponse(String accountId, String holderName, BigDecimal balance) {}

// Mapping in the service:
public AccountResponse toResponse(Account a) {
    return new AccountResponse(a.getId(), a.getHolderName(), a.getBalance());
}
```

### How do SOLID principles tie everything together?

| Principle | How it applies |
|---|---|
| Single Responsibility | TransferService moves money, AuditService records, FeeStrategy computes fees |
| Open/Closed | New fee strategy = new class, zero edits to existing code |
| Liskov Substitution | Any FeeStrategy implementation works where the interface is expected |
| Interface Segregation | FeeStrategy has one method, not a fat interface |
| Dependency Inversion | TransferService depends on the FeeStrategy interface, not a concrete class |

```java
// Dependency Inversion in action – inject via constructor:
public class TransferService {
    private final Map<String, FeeStrategy> feeStrategies;

    public TransferService(Map<String, FeeStrategy> feeStrategies) {
        this.feeStrategies = feeStrategies; // depends on interface, not concrete
    }

    // In a test: new TransferService(Map.of("regular", new RegularFeeStrategy()))
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is a god class that handles transfers, fee calculation, SMS, and audit all in one method. The second mistake is a giant if-else chain for fee types instead of the Strategy pattern. The third is returning entities instead of DTOs.

```java
// Wrong – god method:
public void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
    // calculate fee with if-else
    // move money
    // send SMS
    // write audit
    // update dashboard
    // 500 lines in one method
}

// Correct – separated responsibilities:
transferService.execute(request);      // Strategy for fee, Repository for persistence
// Observer pattern handles SMS, audit, dashboard via event listeners
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

These patterns reinforce each other. Strategy plus a Map registry implements Open/Closed and Dependency Inversion simultaneously. Observer keeps the transfer code stable as new reactions are added. Circuit Breaker prevents one sick dependency from freezing the whole service. DTO protects the API boundary. And SOLID is the foundation that makes all of them clean, testable, and maintainable. In a banking interview, showing how these patterns solve specific pains in real code is what distinguishes a senior answer from a textbook recitation.
